#                                             Economy
@economic_direction
new-page: true
on-arrival: {!
    Q.economic_direction = 'Three Points'; 
    if (Q.state_plan > Q.three_points) {
        Q.economic_direction = 'Returning to Phelps'; 
    }
    if (Q.state_plan > Q.inducing_enterprise) {
        Q.economic_direction = 'Inducing Enterprise'; 
    }
!}
face-image: img/interiors/ipsco.jpg
# face-image: img/Williams_picnic.jpg

Traditionally, the CCF(SS) has pursued a three-pronged economic policy designed to foster a mixed economy. It involves empowering the state corporations and cooperatives of all types while leaving room open for private enterprise. Over the years, provincial capital has been seen by the party bureaucracy as insufficient to develop the province at the pace of neighbouring provinces. Thus, the party has moderated it's stance on private enterprise with business still controlling a large portion of the province's economy, most notably, mineral development.

Current Economic Direction : [+ economic_direction +]

- @three_point
- @state_plan
- @inducing_enterprise
- @path: Go Back

@three_point
title: Continue Advocating for the Three-Pronged Approach 
subtitle: Our traditional economic direction
on-arrival: three_points += 1

@state_plan
title: Revitalize the Socialization Efforts of Joe Phelps, former Cabinet Member in Industry and Resources
subtitle: A rapid expansion of Crown Corporations in the Mineral and Manufacturing Sector <br> - This is highly unpopular with the party elite who see Phelp's state-directed economic policy as brash and disproven
on-arrival: ccf_socialism += 3; state_plan += 1; managerial_dissent += 10

@inducing_enterprise
title: Private Capital has played a key role in the province's development, we should not disregard the benefits of them
subtitle: Attempt to develop the province's economy at a faster rate with generous incentives to private enterprise
on-arrival: ccf_socialism -= 5; inducing_enterprise += 1; ccf_socialism -= 5; socialism_support -= 3

# _______________________________________________________________________________________
#                                           Labour 

@northern_direction
new-page: true
face-image: img/misc_photos/cree_women.jpg

The North is historically the poorest and geographically isolated region of Saskatchewan. Populated by an indigenous majority, the North has proven an enigma to our party. Under the Douglas administration, efforts to centralize the northern economy under Crown Corporations saw marginal price increases for local fishers and hunters but little else. 

In the last years of the Douglas government, plans were made to convert the northern Crown Corporations into co-operatives to better service northern residents. Necessarily, this means that we will lack much the economic clout we have to influence the northern economy. But de-centralization may well prove to handle the situation better. 

- @centralize_north_direction
- @decentralize_north_direction
- @path: Go Back

@centralize_north_direction
title: Halt the conversion of the Northern Crown Corporations
subtitle: This risks alienating segments of the Indigenous population who have long complained of government overreach

@decentralize_north_direction
title: Continue with the conversions of the Northern Crowns into co-operatives
subtitle: This will leave us less room to direct the northern economy


